🟢 Level 1 — Beginner (Foundations)
You know Linux if you can:
Use basic commands:
ls, cd, pwd, mkdir, rm, cp, mv
View file contents:
cat, less, head, tail
Understand:
Directory structure (/home, /etc, /bin, /usr)
File permissions (rwx)
Use:
sudo
Package managers (apt, dnf, pacman)
Install software from terminal
If this is you → You understand Linux basics.
🟡 Level 2 — Intermediate (Practical Usage)
You know Linux well if you can:
Modify file permissions:
chmod, chown
Use process commands:
ps, top, htop, kill
Use networking tools:
ping, curl, ssh
Edit files using:
vim or nano
Understand:
Shell scripting basics
Environment variables
Manage services:
systemctl
Use pipes and redirection:
|, >, >>
If this is you → You are comfortable working in Linux environments.
🔵 Level 3 — Advanced (Engineer Level)
You really know Linux if you can:
Write shell scripts with:
loops, conditions, functions
Manage:
Users & groups
Cron jobs
Understand:
File system types (ext4, xfs)
Mounting & partitions
Use:
grep, awk, sed
Configure:
SSH, firewall
Debug performance issues
Compile programs from source
If this is you → You are Linux proficient.
🔴 Level 4 — Power User / DevOps Level
You deeply understand Linux if you can:
Kernel concepts (process scheduling, memory management)
Docker & containers
System logs debugging
Write advanced Bash scripts
Configure servers (Nginx, Apache)
Automate deployments
Tune performance
If this is you → You think like a Linux systems engineer.
